Id: no-old-owner-for-this-repository
Pattern: iderex/jellyfin-plugin-discover
Subject: *.md *.cs *.html *.csproj *.props *.sln *.json *.txt *.sh *.ps1 *.yml *.yaml
Except: none
Issue: 227

This repository moved between accounts and the tree went on naming the old one.
Sixty eight occurrences across seven files carried the superseded address, and
twenty one files carried the current one, at the commit before the sweep that
brought this rule:

    git grep -o 'iderex/jellyfin-plugin-discover' 049d0bd | wc -l
    68
    git grep -l 'iderex/jellyfin-plugin-discover' 049d0bd | wc -l
    7
    git grep -l 'Flowfin/jellyfin-plugin-discover' 049d0bd | wc -l
    21

The split was by the date a page was written rather than by the page, so every
recently written page was right and every older one was wrong, and nothing in a
review of either one would show it. That is the shape a rule is for and a reading
is not: the two spellings otherwise return a page at a time, for as long as
anybody keeps writing pages.

The pattern is the full repository name rather than the account, and the
difference is load bearing. This paragraph rested it on three lines of
`docs/gate-parity.md`, which read the gate on a different repository in the same
older account, and said a rule matching the account would take those with it.
That page names the gate at its current owner now, so the example is gone:

    git grep -c 'iderex/jellyfin-plugin-sso' -- docs/gate-parity.md ; echo "exit=$?"
    exit=1

The reason is not gone with it, and this is the half to read rather than the
count. An account-wide pattern refuses an address because of who used to hold
it, not because the address is wrong, so it reaches every other board this tree
quotes and refuses a correct one the day that board's page is written under the
old account for a good reason. That it would refuse nothing today is a fact about
this week's corpus rather than about the pattern.

The subject is the file types this tree's text is written in: the documentation
and the changelog, the source, the configuration page, the project and solution
files, the scripts and the workflows. A rule file is none of them, so the pattern
does not reach the place it is written down.

Nothing is excepted. Every address of this repository in tracked text is the
current one, and no file needs the old spelling to say what it means. The five
recorded commands and the four run links that carried it were each re-run under
the current spelling before they moved, and each answered the same, so what moved
was an address and not a claim. Those runs are in the pull request that landed
this.

What it cannot do. It reads tracked text one line at a time, so an address
assembled from pieces, or built from a variable a workflow supplies at run time,
is invisible to it. It says nothing about whether the old address still resolves:
a redirect is a property of the account rather than of this tree, and it holds
only while the old account has no repository of that name, which is a condition
nothing in this checkout can read. And it judges this tree rather than the
tracker, so an issue body, a pull request body or a commit message naming the old
address is outside every pathspec above and stays where it was written.
